WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:06.529
Movies with shared continuity are all the
rage these days, and you've got one movie

00:00:06.529 --> 00:00:11.780
franchise to thank: Iron Man. When the Robert
Downey Jr.-starring action flick debuted in

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:16.390
2008, no one could've predicted the effect
it would have on movies for years afterwards.

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:20.040
Marvel Studios and director Jon Favreau laid
the early groundwork for the Marvel Cinematic

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:24.340
Universe by stuffing in as many Easter eggs
and hidden references as they could, so for

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:29.800
obsessive comic fans, the Iron Man franchise
can be summed up in one word: Marvelous.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:31.439
The Dude survives

00:00:31.439 --> 00:00:35.110
Jeff Bridges' performance as bad guy Obadiah
Stane in the original Iron Man may have been

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:38.970
just fine, but everyone knows it wasn't the
role for which he'll always be known. No,

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:43.170
that distinction belongs to The Dude from
The Big Lebowski. As it turns out, the effects

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:47.930
guys on Iron Man agreed, and added small tributes
to The Dude in Stane's secret computer files,

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:51.740
which feature fine-print dialogue from The
Big Lebowski. Additionally, the weapons Stane

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:56.130
sent to the bad guys went on a ship called
"Lebowski." Those details really tied the

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.010
scene together, man.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:00:59.760
Urdu, or don't

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.190
In the beginning of Iron Man, Tony Stark is
kidnapped by a terrorist group known as The

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:07.909
Ten Rings. Stark's captors discuss their plan,
and their secret partnership with Tony's friend,

00:01:07.909 --> 00:01:12.390
Stane, right in front of him, spoiling the
whole movie before it can even really begin…but

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:16.920
only if you speak Urdu, the national language
of Pakistan. Most Western audiences were none

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:20.060
the wiser. Speaking of the Ten Rings…

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:21.810
Ten Rings to rule them all

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:25.210
The first Iron Man flick would eventually
spawn a franchise, but the filmmakers didn't

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:28.810
know that at the time. There was no way to
know when, or if, we'd ever really get to

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:33.140
see longtime magic- ring-wearing Iron Man
villain Mandarin make it to the big screen.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:36.869
So instead of featuring the bad guy with handfuls
of bling, the filmmakers instead decided to

00:01:36.869 --> 00:01:40.659
pay tribute to the Mandarin by naming the
terrorists after him. Which is way less cool

00:01:40.659 --> 00:01:42.869
than having a street named after you or something.

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:44.040
That song rings a bell...

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:47.869
Still on the subject of rings, when Tony's
pal Rhodey gets a call from him on his cell

00:01:47.869 --> 00:01:54.950
phone, we can hear a very unique ringtone.
That little tune is actually the theme song

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:59.810
from the 1966 Iron Man cartoon. Black Sabbath's
"Iron Man" probably would've been a little

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:00.560
more badass...

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:02.310
...but maybe a little too on-the-nose.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:05.490
O Captain! My Captain!

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:09.080
Fans saw the first big hints at a larger Marvel
Cinematic Universe when they spotted a familiar

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:13.090
sight on Tony Stark's work bench. Sitting
along with unfinished projects and other junk

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:17.060
is none other than Captain America's famous
patriotic shield. The shield shows up again

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:21.090
in Iron Man 2, while a couple of Captain America
comic books also make an appearance in Howard

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:25.739
Stark's trunk. Both movies predate the legendary
superhero's first cinematic appearance in

00:02:25.739 --> 00:02:28.629
2011's Captain America: The First Avenger.

00:02:28.629 --> 00:02:29.879
Bambi Arbogast

00:02:29.879 --> 00:02:34.489
In the comics, Bambina "Bambi" Arbogast has
been a valued Stark employee for decades.

00:02:34.489 --> 00:02:38.669
During her first appearance in 1979, she helps
Iron Man defeat Whirlwind in the middle of

00:02:38.669 --> 00:02:43.150
her own job interview, before going on to
play a key role in a number of other storylines.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:47.120
You can spot Bambi, played by Margy Moore,
in Iron Man 2, when she’s seen working for

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:51.650
Pepper Potts, the new CEO of Stark Industries.
Bambi's also the one who ends up unwittingly

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:56.209
hiring Black Widow, for which we can all be
grateful. She's also heard offscreen in Iron

00:02:56.209 --> 00:03:00.329
Man 3, reminding Pepper of her appointment
with ultra-creep Aldrich Killian.

00:03:00.329 --> 00:03:01.999
Say hello, Stan

00:03:01.999 --> 00:03:06.169
Spotting Stan Lee's cameos has become a favorite
pastime for Marvel Movie fans, and his appearances

00:03:06.169 --> 00:03:10.699
in the Iron Man movies are some of the best.
In the first film, he shows up as "Hugh Hefner,"

00:03:10.699 --> 00:03:14.870
with a beautiful woman on each arm. In Iron
Man 2, he can be glimpsed as a pretty uncanny

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:19.269
stand-in for legendary talk show host Larry
King. And in Iron Man 3, it's back to the

00:03:19.269 --> 00:03:24.419
babes, as Lee pops up for a moment as a very,
uh, enthusiastic beauty pageant judge. Those

00:03:24.419 --> 00:03:28.559
were probably all way better for his health
than some of his other Marvel Movie cameos...y'know,

00:03:28.559 --> 00:03:30.659
like all those times he almost got killed.

00:03:30.659 --> 00:03:32.159
Lady Man-Thing

00:03:32.159 --> 00:03:36.689
In Iron Man 3, Ellen Brandt is a war veteran
recruited into Aldrich Killian's army of Extremis

00:03:36.689 --> 00:03:40.599
soldiers, and, spoiler alert, she’s killed
in an explosion after attempting to get some

00:03:40.599 --> 00:03:44.879
sensitive documents from Tony Stark. It's
a fairly minor role, but hardcore Marvel fans

00:03:44.879 --> 00:03:48.879
might have recognized Brandt's name from the
comics, where she's the ex-wife of Ted Sallis,

00:03:48.879 --> 00:03:53.120
better known as the Man-Thing. In the original
story, the duplicitous Brandt is actually

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:57.949
responsible for Sallis' swampy transformation,
who attacks and disfigures her in retaliation,

00:03:57.949 --> 00:04:01.909
a detail nodded to in the movie with the scars
on her cheek. Most Marvel fans would agree,

00:04:01.909 --> 00:04:05.069
though, that Ellen Brandt is a pretty obscure
reference. But it's still probably a good

00:04:05.069 --> 00:04:09.449
thing Iron Man 3's screenwriters didn't just
call her Mrs. Man-Thing.

00:04:09.449 --> 00:04:10.839
Mapping the future of Marvel

00:04:10.839 --> 00:04:14.989
When Tony meets with Nick Fury at the end
of Iron Man 2 to discuss the emerging Avengers

00:04:14.989 --> 00:04:18.299
Initiative, viewers can see a map marking
S.H.I.E.L.D.’s locations of interest in

00:04:18.299 --> 00:04:22.170
the background. It's the kind of thing you
need to pause and zoom to really get a good

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:25.680
look at, but it's loaded with pretty obvious
references to the comics.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:27.020
These spots include...

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:30.450
...a location in Africa near the fictional
Wakanda, home of the Black Panther...

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:34.240
...one near Norway where the Tesseract is
found by the Red Skull in Captain America:

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:35.470
The First Avenger...

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:38.370
...another near Greenland where Cap crashes
the Skull's ship…

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:41.240
...and one in New Mexico, where Thor's hammer
had just turned up.

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:45.500
All of this was way back in 2010, before Marvel
really started rolling out its Marvel Cinematic

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:49.370
Universe Phases, so it proves that Marvel’s
planning has been tighter than Black Widow’s

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:51.080
outfit all along.

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:52.150
Hulking out

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:56.240
During the same meeting between Stark and
Fury in Iron Man 2, particularly observant

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:00.060
viewers can see a television in the background,
tuned to a news report being delivered live

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:04.170
from the campus at Culver University. Not
coincidentally, Culver is where the Hulk faced

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:08.040
off against Thunderbolt Ross and Emil Blonsky
in The Incredible Hulk, which puts the events

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:12.530
of that film as happening in the same time
frame as Iron Man 2 in the Marvel Movie timeline...

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:16.560
but still a few years before Tony would deliver
the ol’ Hulkbuster jackhammer to Hulk’s face.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:20.300
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to see more videos like the one you

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:23.530
just saw. And leave us a comment to let us
know which hidden Iron Man secret was your

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:24.030
favorite...

